+Because of those three differences, naive workarounds still break down at scale. Appending a local exception avoids debugging altogether, adding coupling and accelerating store bloat until further additions stop improving the system ([raw accumulation does not create usable memory](./raw-accumulation-does-not-create-usable-memory.md)). Trusting an agent's prose rationale guesses blame without checking causal faithfulness. Exhaustive one-by-one ablation verifies causality cleanly on small stores, but becomes prohibitively expensive once hundreds of artifacts interact.
 
 Scaling credit assignment therefore turns on making the retained store **debuggable**—separating cheap suspect narrowing from targeted causal checks, just as software engineering does. Execution traces recording which instructions, retrieved notes, and scripts actually loaded narrow suspects from the whole store to the active slice. [Near-decomposability](./near-decomposability-may-make-local-inspection-affordable.md) and explicit dependency links bound how many artifacts can interact. When rules rest on an explicit [addressable theory](./definitions/addressable-theory.md) with stated predictions, a failed prediction points to candidate premises to check, though it rarely isolates a single faulty one. Replaying the failing case with a shortlisted entry withheld or modified verifies causal responsibility directly, and codifying settled rules into deterministic scripts moves them back into ordinary software debugging. Debugging will not match backpropagation's scale across billions of coupled parameters, but over a modular store of hundreds or thousands of artifacts it gives a plausible path to fully automated credit assignment.
 
